// fire — the first kernel whose appearance is not a curve over age.
//
// An ember cools, soots up and blows thin as it flies, getting wider as it
// thins; when it lands it cools fast, dumps its carbon, and is squeezed back
// toward dense so it reads as a mark rather than a puff.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THAT LOOKS LIKE IS NOT IN THIS FILE. It writes no colour: it writes a
// POSITION IN AN APPEARANCE MANIFOLD — three state channels the renderer reads
// an RBF set at (funideas §9) — and the set named `"fire"` lives with the
// spray, not here. Whether this reads as a campfire or a welding spark is
// decided there.
//
// Age is still in here — it is what `perish` reads — but nothing about how the
// row LOOKS comes from it. What the row looks like is wherever its state got
// to, and the state is pushed around by the world.
//
//   row.u0  cooled   0 white-hot ... 1 cold
//   row.u1  sooted   0 clean ....... 1 all carbon
//   row.u2  thinned  0 dense ....... 1 blown thin
//
// All three are born at ZERO, which is not a coincidence: a `spawn`
// zeroes a row's user channels, so the axes are spelled as how much has
// HAPPENED to the row, never as what it has left. A newborn has no
// history, and (0, 0, 0) is the flame.
//
// Every line is a `relax` — the step toward a target at a rate per
// SECOND, which saturates, so no influence can drive a channel past its
// target and no clamp is needed to say so. They are all `write … add`,
// and a row's queued adds land on the live value in order, so the
// influences SUM. That is the whole trick. A second cooling term is not
// a special case of the first, it is another force on the same channel.
//
// The knobs are rates per second (`relax` eats the fed dt). The first
// draft of this kernel spelled each line `| mul -1 | add 1 | mul <rate>`
// and the rates were per TICK — correct at one dt and quietly wrong at
// every other. That kernel is what `relax` was built for.
// Every `plane.drift.@self.k.<name>` below is a KNOB, not a value: this file
// says what to turn and the number itself is seeded from outside. `drift-run`
// seeds only `gravity`; everything else needs a `--seed` of its own, and a
// knob that never arrives is REFUSED BY NAME at the row rather than read as
// zero. So:
//
//   drift-run --kernel kernels/fire.rill \\
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.gravity =<cells/s²>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.cool    =<per s, in air>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.plunge  =<per s, on contact>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.chill   =<per s, once thin>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.smoke   =<per s, carbon in flight>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.quench  =<per s, carbon dumped on landing>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.thin    =<per s, spreading>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.settle  =<per s, pulled back dense>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.puff    =<extra radius when thin>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.grain   =<base radius, cells>
//
// ⚠ A `relax` RATE has a ceiling: a step that would close more than the whole
// gap is refused, so any rate above 1/dt refuses rather than clamping — above
// 60 at 60 Hz, above 10 on a 100 ms headless tick. "Snuff it instantly" is not
// a big number here; it is a refusal.
//
// Units: `row.size` is a RADIUS in cells, `gravity` is cells/s² on y and
// NEGATIVE is down (a positive pull lifts).

describe fire
    "An ember that changes substance as it flies: white-hot and dense at birth, cooling, sooting up and spreading wider as it blows thin. It lands flat, stops dead and is squeezed tight again, so what is left on the floor is a dark mark rather than a puff."

spawn
gravity plane.drift.@self.k.gravity
collide | stick
perish

// -- cooled ------------------------------------------------------------
// It cools on its own,
row.u0 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.cool | write row.u0 add
// faster against something it landed on (the cold plate),
row.u0 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.plunge | mul row.stuck | write row.u0 add
// and faster once it is thin enough for the air to get at it.
row.u0 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.chill | mul row.u2 | write row.u0 add

// -- sooted ------------------------------------------------------------
// Carbon accumulates as it burns,
row.u1 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.smoke | write row.u1 add
// and a quench dumps it: incomplete combustion is what a soot mark IS.
row.u1 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.quench | mul row.stuck | write row.u1 add

// -- thinned -----------------------------------------------------------
// It spreads as it goes. Named `thin` for the channel it drives, and NOT
// because `spread` would collide: that was true once — naming it `spread`
// retuned the spray's launch cone to 0.013 cells/s while the mount line
// still printed the 1.6 the flag asked for — and the `.k.` room exists
// precisely to end it. `@self.k.spread` and `@self.spread` are different
// paths now, and `kindle.rill` uses the former with no ill effect.
row.u2 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.thin | write row.u2 add
// A stuck row is pulled back toward dense, so a soot mark stays a mark.
// It does not reach zero: `thin` is not gated on being free, so the two
// lines BALANCE at thin/(thin + settle) rather than cancelling. Gating
// it would want two saturating factors in one flow, which a row cannot
// spell today.
row.u2 | relax 0 plane.drift.@self.k.settle | mul row.stuck | write row.u2 add

// The row's SIZE is state too, not a curve: a thin row is a wide one.
// (funideas §9 — the particle morphs its whole rendering model.)
row.u2 | mul plane.drift.@self.k.puff | add plane.drift.@self.k.grain | write row.size
